### Tested versions

- Reproducible in: 4.7.dev (commit 1aabcb9e9b)

### System information

N/A — source-level bug, affects all platforms in release builds.

### Issue description

`Callable::callp()` and `Callable::rpcp()` in `core/variant/callable.cpp` crash with a null pointer dereference when invoked on a freed object **in release builds**. The null check guarding `ObjectDB::get_instance()` is wrapped in `#ifdef DEBUG_ENABLED`, so it is compiled out in release/export templates.

This means any deferred call, signal emission, or RPC to an object that has been freed will silently crash the application with no error message or diagnostic.

**`callp()` — lines 59-70:**
```cpp
} else {
    Object *obj = ObjectDB::get_instance(ObjectID(object));
#ifdef DEBUG_ENABLED
    if (!obj) {
        r_call_error.error = CallError::CALL_ERROR_INSTANCE_IS_NULL;
        r_call_error.argument = 0;
        r_call_error.expected = 0;
        r_return_value = Variant();
        return;
    }
#endif
    r_return_value = obj->callp(method, p_arguments, p_argcount, r_call_error);
}
```

**`rpcp()` — lines 95-117:**
```cpp
} else if (!is_custom()) {
    Object *obj = ObjectDB::get_instance(ObjectID(object));
#ifdef DEBUG_ENABLED
    if (!obj || !obj->is_class("Node")) {
        ...
        return ERR_UNCONFIGURED;
    }
#endif
    ...
    Error err = (Error)obj->callp(SNAME("rpc_id"), argptrs, argcount, tmp).operator int64_t();
```

Both functions dereference `obj` without a guard in release builds. `ObjectDB::get_instance()` returns `nullptr` when the object has been freed (its ObjectID is no longer valid).

### Steps to reproduce

This is a source-level bug. The issue can be triggered in any release/export build when:

1. A `Callable` is created targeting an object.
2. The target object is freed (e.g., via `queue_free()` or `free()`).
3. The callable is later invoked (e.g., a deferred call or signal emission fires after the object was freed).

In debug builds, this correctly returns `CALL_ERROR_INSTANCE_IS_NULL`. In release builds, it crashes.

### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)

N/A — this is a code correctness issue visible from source analysis. The affected code path is every `Callable` invocation on a freed object in a release/export build.
